1
00:00:00,230 --> 00:00:14,670
Full of Surprises

2
00:00:14,670 --> 00:00:17,610
The mailman just dropped some mail in our box, Grandpa.

3
00:00:17,610 --> 00:00:21,280
Probably a lot of advertising and bills.

4
00:00:21,280 --> 00:00:22,840
Why don't you write to me, Robbie,

5
00:00:22,840 --> 00:00:46,170
so I can get some interesting mail?

6
00:00:46,170 --> 00:00:48,150
You were right, Grandpa.

7
00:00:48,150 --> 00:00:53,820
Advertising, bills, bills, advertising.

8
00:00:53,820 --> 00:00:56,680
Yes. It's just like I said, Robbie.

9
00:00:56,680 --> 00:00:58,260
Nothing interesting.

10
00:00:58,260 --> 00:01:00,450
You won't believe it, Grandpa,

11
00:01:00,450 --> 00:01:02,650
but there's a letter here addressed to you,

12
00:01:02,650 --> 00:01:05,360
Mr. Malcolm Stewart, and it looks like a personal letter.

13
00:01:05,360 --> 00:01:06,900
Oh, it must be a bill.

14
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:08,340
I don't think so.

15
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:10,010
The return name and address is

16
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:13,560
Pete Waters, RFD Number 1, Chesterton.

17
00:01:13,560 --> 00:01:17,310
You're joking. Pete Waters?

18
00:01:17,310 --> 00:01:20,920
Pete Waters, RFD Number 1, Chesterton.

19
00:01:20,920 --> 00:01:22,080
You know him?

20
00:01:22,080 --> 00:01:24,740
Do I know Pete Waters?

21
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:26,630
You bet I do!

22
00:01:26,630 --> 00:01:33,890
He was my roommate in college.

23
00:01:33,890 --> 00:01:36,040
He visited with Grandma and me in Florida

24
00:01:36,040 --> 00:01:44,680
about five years ago.

25
00:01:44,680 --> 00:01:45,930
What does he say?

26
00:01:45,930 --> 00:01:47,970
Is he OK?

27
00:01:47,970 --> 00:01:52,490
Yeah, he's fine.

28
00:01:52,490 --> 00:01:53,110
Just fine.

29
00:01:53,110 --> 00:01:53,910
He's writing to invite me

30
00:01:53,910 --> 00:01:57,310
to spend a weekend with him at his farm.

31
00:01:57,310 --> 00:01:58,660
He's planning a get-together

32
00:01:58,660 --> 00:02:01,570
with two or three other college friends.

33
00:02:01,570 --> 00:02:06,410
A kind of a fifty-year anniversary reunion.

34
00:02:06,410 --> 00:02:08,550
Sounds like fun.

35
00:02:08,550 --> 00:02:10,780
Fifty years? Wow!

36
00:02:10,780 --> 00:02:13,700
It sounds like fun to me, too, Robbie.

37
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,910
What kind of farm does he have?

38
00:02:15,910 --> 00:02:18,030
I've never been there, Robbie,

39
00:02:18,030 --> 00:02:22,230
but he has chickens and cows and all.

40
00:02:22,230 --> 00:02:25,150
That means fresh eggs and fresh milk.

41
00:02:25,150 --> 00:02:27,220
Does he have a family?

42
00:02:27,220 --> 00:02:29,170
No, he doesn't, Robbie.

43
00:02:29,170 --> 00:02:30,890
He never married.

44
00:02:30,890 --> 00:02:32,920
He's not as lucky as I am

45
00:02:32,920 --> 00:02:37,050
to have a family and grandchildren.

46
00:02:37,050 --> 00:02:38,910
I'm a lucky man.

47
00:02:38,910 --> 00:02:41,390
How come he never got married?

48
00:02:41,390 --> 00:02:43,460
That's a good question, Robbie.

49
00:02:43,460 --> 00:02:45,830
A very good question.

50
00:02:45,830 --> 00:02:47,940
He never married because

51
00:02:47,940 --> 00:02:51,390
the girl he was in love with in college married someone else.

52
00:02:51,390 --> 00:02:53,270
As simple as that.

53
00:02:53,270 --> 00:02:55,320
He never got over it.

54
00:02:55,320 --> 00:02:57,230
He must have loved her very much.

55
00:02:57,230 --> 00:03:00,450
Yes. Very much.

56
00:03:00,450 --> 00:03:02,250
Lillian Winters.

57
00:03:02,250 --> 00:03:03,910
She was in our class.

58
00:03:03,910 --> 00:03:05,460
And what happened?

59
00:03:05,460 --> 00:03:07,950
She was in love with Donald McGrath,

60
00:03:07,950 --> 00:03:10,430
the quarterback on our football team.

61
00:03:10,430 --> 00:03:14,280
Football players are always popular with the ladies.

62
00:03:14,280 --> 00:03:18,240
She liked Pete, and they went to dances together.

63
00:03:18,240 --> 00:03:20,820
But her heart was with Donald.

64
00:03:20,820 --> 00:03:23,170
Did he ever get over it?

65
00:03:23,170 --> 00:03:23,560
No, he never did.

66
00:03:23,560 --> 00:03:26,730
Where is she today?

67
00:03:26,730 --> 00:03:29,070
I don't know.

68
00:03:29,070 --> 00:03:31,460
Maybe Lillian will be at the reunion.

69
00:03:31,460 --> 00:03:32,920
You think so?

70
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:34,850
Pete's full of surprises.

71
00:03:34,850 --> 00:03:37,260
I wish I could go there with you, Grandpa.

72
00:03:37,260 --> 00:03:40,440
What do you think the surprise will be?

73
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:43,330
With Pete, you never know, Robbie.

74
00:03:43,330 --> 00:03:47,390
Won't it be exciting to see all your college friends there again?

75
00:03:47,390 --> 00:03:49,890
It is already.

76
00:03:49,890 --> 00:03:54,790
I'm kind of excited about going now.

77
00:03:54,790 --> 00:03:56,550
Next weekend ...

78
00:03:56,550 --> 00:03:59,690
sleep over Friday and Saturday night

79
00:03:59,690 --> 00:04:02,530
and come back Sunday.

80
00:04:02,530 --> 00:04:06,370
I can't wait!

81
00:04:06,370 --> 00:04:07,890
Don't you think you ought to call Pete

82
00:04:07,890 --> 00:04:09,380
and tell him you're coming?

83
00:04:09,380 --> 00:04:12,380
You're reading my mind, Robbie.
